the situation is dire Emperor Zayn seeks  to slake his thirst for revenge with the  heart of an Curia in the city of alias a  small group of heroes must thus stand  against an entire empire the time for  resistance is now  hi there it's kevin van ORD standing in  for reviewer brett todd to tell you  about sacred 3 what's in a name if you  take the example of sacred 3 pretty much  nothing this game may bear the name of  an action role-playing series but it's  actually more of a half-hearted gauntlet  clone this mediocre button masher strips  out just about everything that makes  click fest rpgs worth clicking around in  a script inspired by equal measures of  Tolkien and Leisure Suit Larry is the  first big problem at first it seems like  you're stepping into the brawny boots of  the usual hero in the usual elves and  orcs saga the fate of the realm of  Arcania is at stake here and Lord Zayn  and his ashen empire are pillaging towns  innocents are being slaughtered undead  are rising weirdly enough though these  events are all played for laughs this is  one of the most aggressively obnoxious  games in recent times comments by  friends and foes alike consist of out of  place observations and terrible sex  jokes that even beavis and butthead  would find embarrassing  was it good for you too you can never  escape this chatter either as you're  constantly accompanied by a veritable  broadcast booth of idiots don't look to  the gameplay to make up for the scripts  deficiencies on the contrary the game is  stripped down to the raw basics  there are just four different types of  characters to choose from at the start  of the game all fantasy archetypes none  can be customized before delving into  the campaign either alone or with three  others it's easy to find a match to drop  into which you can do with just a couple  of clicks from the main campaign screen  you can also readily move in and out of  online and offline play so the option  for something different is always there  adding other players doesn't  dramatically change how you play however  sacred 3 is just the same old same old  whether you're on your own or playing  with others  character progression is weak there are  no loot drops during combat and there's  no inventory kill a bad guy and you're  showered with gold and shining orbs that  boost health and power but you never  earn any artifacts like magical swords  or ancient Helms instead the game doles  this stuff out to you like rations as  rewards for finishing missions and  leveling up without the instant  gratification provided by cool new gear  and enchanted artifacts all of the  clicking is just a pointless grind yay  our troops are helping the ones you  freed keep doing what you're doing as a  result the entire game is played with  just three or four weapons there are  limited slots for amenities like health  potions and shield amulets each hero  comes with default attacks plus shared  special abilities like a bash that can  take down enemy shields and an execution  move that finishes stunned opponents  only a handful of combat arts provide  heroes with added class specific  abilities most of the above can be  upgraded by purchasing branches on skill  trees that offer up added damage wider  attack range and so forth but they're  not easy to come by and some are so far  off in the distance that they seem  unattainable especially in the early  stages of the game  having to get to level 41 to unlock the  barbarians final armor buff which is  just the fourth available is just crazy  sacred three consists of disconnected  levels and wholly separate locales and  dungeons all of which are rigidly linear  enemies are repeated over and over again  in both the story and arena levels much  of the enemy horde consists of goblin  like monsters giant spiders trolls and  the like although occasionally you get  thrown a curveball like gross puking  zombies and ice creatures and when the  rogues gallery gets changed up most of  the differences are only skin-deep as  all enemies seem to rely on just a few  types of attacks  there are some positives here boss  battles at the end of each story level  can be very challenging they often  require you to take advantage of the  scenery to survive or use some arcade  styled gimmick to turn the enemy against  itself battles are frantic whirls of  color showing off some attractive  visuals that fill the screen up with  explosions and magic effects levels are  equally nice to look at with varied  backdrops that range from jungles to  crypts to quasi factory settings sacred  threes biggest problem is that it's  simply so forgettable  it isn't just removed from the previous  sacred games it's all so far removed  from the reasons you come to games like  this in the first place  oh boy  I beloved Gloria we destroyed 20 cities  together
 

